A||N||8||Shadow||Major Steering Wheel Vibration||||8||
Z||000000||Shadow||10-31-2000||04:12 PM||||<BR>     I've had this problem for a while and the shop guys always said that my vibration in the steering wheel was caused by an out of round tire.  I just bought new tires and the vibration is still there.  The Vibration comes at all speeds.  I lifted the front end of the car up to see if anything is loose and give everything a good shake, nothing there either.  There was a little bit of play with the rack and pinion steering though on the passengers side, enough to make a click.  Any help will be appreciated. <BR>Thanks <BR>Trevor ||||reg||8
Z||000001||TheFiero64||10-31-2000||04:44 PM||||If you also had the tires balanced then I can only think it might be worn suspension bushings causing the A-Arms to float. Anyone know if I may be on target?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||3
Z||000002||bHooper||10-31-2000||04:47 PM||||it could be a number of things (duh).  how did the tires ware on the front?  if they were even, that would eliminate the struts... could be something like the tie rod ends, or the ball joints.<P>it could possibly be the bushing in the steering rack, on the passenger side.  if you tug on it and it makes a "click" that would be my guess.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||Shadow||10-31-2000||04:50 PM||||The Front tires wore evenly and the shock/struts are somewhat new.  If I can find a bushing at the local part shop I might give that a try.||||reg||11
Z||000004||gixxer||10-31-2000||06:33 PM||||If you can detect any freeplay at all, the bushing is worn, and will cause all sorts of weird and lively feedback thru the steering wheel. <BR>Try Mr. Dickmans site <A HREF="http://rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>http://rodneydickman.com</A>  <BR>He has your bushing.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||stsmithgt||11-01-2000||12:40 PM||||Nobody has mentioned anything about the rim having the possibility of being out of round, warped, bent....  When I worked at a Mazda dealership, I could tell by the balance of the wheel if it was on the front or the back (new vehicles before any tire rotations have been done).  Obviously the fronts take more abuse steering the car around.  This tends to get the rims out of shape. Shoot, an inexperienced guy putting the tires on could screw up the rim enough to give you a vibration. OK ,NEXT OPINION, POSSIBLILITY OR SUGGESTION. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||11-01-2000||12:48 PM||||I'm going with bad alignment. Even though the tires wore evenly, I still stick with it. I've seen cars(driven one)that wore the tires extremly un-evenly and it rode very smooth. Possible they wore evenly, even though the alignment is off. I also don't think the steering rack bushing could cause this problem, unless it is considerably worn. 4-wheel alignment is my bet.||||reg||1
Z||000007||tgowens||11-01-2000||01:06 PM||||just curious, have you checked the steering column itself? suspension is a funny thing, start with the wheels, move to the control arms and the steering rack, then to the column itself -- vibration could be coming from any of those components. I have seen steering columns loose that caused that type of vibration........<P>------------------<BR>               <IMG SRC="http://www.tgowens.com/tgowens_sig.gif">                           <BR><A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero" TARGET=_blank>www.tgowens.com/fiero</A> - Blue '87 GT - Gold '87 GT<BR><A HREF="http://www.cfog.org" TARGET=_blank>www.cfog.org</A> - Classic Fiero Owners Group<BR><A HREF="http://www.http://www.fieroshop.net/" TARGET=_blank>www.http://www.fieroshop.net/</A> - Fiero Shop - Richmond, CA - 1-510-23-FIERO||||reg||1
Z||000008||286SE'S||11-01-2000||03:21 PM||||I had a nasty shake when I got my car and it was the passenger side wheel bearing. If your car is NOT and 88 then there is a "steering damper" strut on the rack. Take it off and see if is still working (good firm action).<BR>I replaced mine and got a noticable improvement (but not elimination) in the shake before the wheel bearing was done.<BR>||||reg||1
